  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Food security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">(in year 2) AFF delivered 345 additional climate resilient garden beds, and 90 per cent of beneficiaries showed sustained use of garden beds to grow vegetables. Training sessions on construction and use of climate resilient agricultural technologies, composting, seed saving, preparation and preservation of vegetables were attended by a total of 622 beneficiaries (56 per cent women).  Overall, 33 different vegetable crops were grown, and 23 per cent of beneficiaries reported growing more than half of their vegetable needs.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Food security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Atolls Food Futures Program (AFF) aims to improve resilience and food security of vulnerable households through increased production and consumption of locally grown nutritious foods, particularly fresh vegetables, using a suite of innovative climate smart technologies delivered through a community-based approach with a strong focus on household-level intervention, local food systems and community ownership. AFF has delivered all planned activities for this year- so far, the program has distributed over 1,173 Foodcubes, 330sqm of keyhole gardens and 170 and other climate smart agriculture technologies. Training as been delivered across more than 20 Pacific communities, directly benefitting over 4,000 households.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Food security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Growing food is challenging in atolls due to poor soil, limited land and water availability and increasingly, saltwater intrusion. Atoll food Futures project has supported 739 smallholder farmers in Tuvalu, Kiribati and Fiji with knowledge, skills and technology to grow vegetables and fruit, including composting and use of raised wicking garden beds. 
&#x200d;
Residents of Papaelise (a Tuvaluan islet off Fongafale where Funafuti is located), including Tianamo Lusia, have depended on the sea and travelling to Fongafale to access food and basic provisions. Though support from AFF, Tianamo now not only produces enough for his family to eat, but also for sale back on the mainland. &#x201c;I do not depend on Fongafale anymore. I save a lot not going to the store. I function as a middleman between customers on Fongafale and my farming neighbours. I also sell at the monthly Tau-maketi and by the roadside on Funafuti. I use e-marketing on Facebook , people contact me there to buy. I usually sell pawpaw, pumpkin, bananas and breadfruit. Depending on the produce, I can earn up to $1000 a week. I used to sell my fish, but now I do not have to go out when the weather is bad.&quot;</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Food security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Pacific Islands Rural and Agriculture Stimulus Facility (PIRAS) is a collaboration between the International Fund for Agricultural Development (IFAD) and DFAT to support economic recovery of rural farmers from the COVID-19 pandemic in the Pacific, through improved income generation, food security and nutrition for rural communities. PIRAS delivered all planned activities for this reporting year and exceeded its original three-year beneficiaries target (20,200) reaching 33,351 people. This year PIRAS trained 1,495 beneficiaries in improved farming practices, provided land preparation support for 3,095 beneficiaries and provided seeds, seedlings and vegetative planting materials and labour-saving tools and trainings for over 6,500 beneficiaries.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
